    "faq": [
        {
            "question": "What are the key differences between AG Grid and TanStack Table?",
            "answer": "AG Grid renders a complete grid UI with built-in virtualisation, editing and export. TanStack Table is headless: it provides sorting, filtering, grouping and other table logic, and you write all the markup and CSS. TanStack Table's built-in features do not include pivoting, CSV or Excel export, or charts."
        },
        {
            "question": "Why use a TanStack table?",
            "answer": "TanStack Table is used when a team wants full control over how a table looks and behaves. It supplies table state and APIs for features such as sorting, filtering and grouping, and leaves rendering to the developer."
        },
        {
            "question": "Is TanStack Table free?",
            "answer": "Yes. TanStack Table is open source under the MIT licence. TanStack offers paid Enterprise Support as a separate service, with pricing on request."
        },
        {
            "question": "What is the difference between a grid and a table?",
            "answer": "In JavaScript libraries, a table usually displays rows and columns, while a data grid also handles interaction such as sorting, filtering, editing and keyboard navigation. TanStack Table provides the logic for these features and leaves rendering to you; AG Grid provides both."
        },
        {
            "question": "Can I use AG Grid for free?",
            "answer": "Yes. AG Grid Community is free and open source under the MIT licence, including for commercial use. AG Grid Enterprise is a paid licence that adds features such as row grouping, pivoting, Excel export and the Server-Side Row Model. See [Community vs Enterprise](./community-vs-enterprise/)."
        },
        {
            "question": "Does AG Grid include virtualisation and cell editing?",
            "answer": "Yes. AG Grid Community includes row and column virtualisation and built-in cell editors, free under the MIT licence. TanStack Table has neither built in: it pairs with the separate TanStack Virtual package for virtualisation, and its documentation points to TanStack Form for editable cells."
        }
    ],
